Lateral connections between encoder and decoder enable higher denoising autoencoder (dAE) layers to focus on invariant representations. Unlike standard autoencoders, which must pass detailed information through all layers, these connections relieve that burden. Specifically, when invariant features modulate the connection strength, abstract features can guide detailed reconstruction.

Three dAE architectures—with modulated lateral connections, additive lateral connections, and no lateral connections—were tested on real-world images. Experiments confirm that modulated lateral connections:
1) Improve the model's probabilistic accuracy, evidenced by better denoising performance;
2) Accelerate the growth of invariance in higher-layer representations;
3) Promote the development of diverse invariant pooling regions.
